WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
four AI agents you can build today that

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:07.320
will save you hours of work every week.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.160
You don't need any technical skills. If

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.920
you can type, you can build them and

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.120
start using them today. We'll do it

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.680
together, step-by-step. For those who

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.840
are new here, I spent more than 20 years

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.640
as a CEO, board member, and investor in

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.580
tech and AI companies, and I use these

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.880
agents in my work every day. They're

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.080
[music] game-changing. But, the agents

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.400
are the easy part. You want to learn not

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.920
just the tools, but the way of thinking

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.440
or the system that changes how you work

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.480
with AI agents forever. And that system

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.840
works on any AI, Claude, Gemini,

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.400
ChatGPT, you pick. We're going to build

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.080
four agents around four pillars. I call

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.560
it four C's framework: coordination,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.600
creativity, clarity, and coaching. Each

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.320
of these pillars make your life less

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.760
chaotic and more productive. So, let's

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.040
get to the first pillar. The first C is

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.080
coordination, because before anything

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.560
else, AI should stop your day from

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.920
getting hijacked. Microsoft studied how

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:09.680
we actually work, and the numbers are

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.320
brutal. The average worker gets 117

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.040
emails a day, and we're interrupted

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:19.200
every 2 minutes by an email, or a

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.560
meeting, or a message. That's 275

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.160
times a day. And by the way, none of

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.440
that counts the social media

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:29.000
interruptions. Our attention gets

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.440
chopped into pieces every day. So, let's

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.520
build a coordination agent that reads

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.800
your inbox, checks the calendar, plans

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.880
your day, and gives you the time and

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.760
attention back. [music] We will start by

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.000
building the email agent first, and then

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.280
we'll add the calendar right after. For

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.560
this walkthrough, I'm using Claude

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.080
Cohaerence. Chat is where you ask

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.560
questions. Cohaerence is where you

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.240
assign work. But, don't get hung up on

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:59.400
the interface. If you use ChatGPT or

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.600
Gemini or any other agent tool, the same

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.800
principles will work. The buttons will

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.880
change, the workflow will not. So, okay.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.726
Here's the Claude app. You can select

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:07.960
the mode,

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:10.160
>> [music]

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:13.400
>> whether you want to be in a chat tab or

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.800
a co-work tab. Select co-work and it

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:19.000
switches you into tasks.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.720
That's where we will build agents. Now,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.840
we want to think about email, so the

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.360
first thing we're going to do is connect

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:29.480
Gmail so the agent can reach and access

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.480
your inbox. You do this from the plus

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.080
menu.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.720
Connectors, add connectors, browse, then

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.080
select. And congratulations, this is

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.920
your first leap from chatbot to agent.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.200
Once Gmail is connected, you no longer

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.840
have to copy emails into your chat. You

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.680
can say, "Summarize my unread emails

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.280
from yesterday." And the agent knows

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.480
where to go and what to do. And by the

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.720
way, if you're little uneasy about

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.480
letting AI into your inbox, that's a

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.120
good sign. Trust your hesitation. When

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.400
you connect Gmail, Google shows you

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.720
exactly what permissions you're giving

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.800
it. Read it. Don't click through it

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:14.720
blindly. For the first demo, I want the

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:17.720
agent to read and draft emails, not send

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.840
them. Now, we write the prompt. Same

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.560
plain English you would type in chat.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.200
The difference is that now you're

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:27.000
handing it a job. The prompt has five

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.360
parts: the job, the tool, the

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.600
categories, the output, and the

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.519
boundary. You could say something like,

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.880
"Review my unread Gmail from the last 24

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:39.840
hours. Sort it into three buckets:

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.040
urgent, informational, and ignore. For

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.959
anything urgent, draft a reply that

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.000
sounds like me. Don't send anything

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:49.519
without my approval." Notice the five

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.480
parts in that one sentence? The job, the

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:53.000
tool, [music]

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.240
the buckets, the output, and the

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.640
boundary. Now, underneath every agent

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.920
runs the same loop. It reasons, it acts,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.840
it looks at the output, and reasons

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.960
again.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:10.120
Researchers have a name for it, react,

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:12.480
reason and act. But, email is only half

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:15.200
of our coordination agent, correct? Once

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.760
we've used the email agent for a few

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.000
days or maybe even a few weeks, we're

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.240
getting comfortable now. We're ready to

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.320
add the second coordination layer,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.000
calendar. Now, for the walk-through,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.919
same path as Gmail, plus menu,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.919
connectors, add connectors. This time,

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:36.760
select Google Calendar to connect to

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:39.800
Google Calendar. Again, I'm not giving

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:42.680
the agent permission to move meetings or

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.800
change my day without my approval.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.120
At least in the beginning. Here's what I

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.040
would prompt. Look at my Google Calendar

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.640
for today and tomorrow.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.000
Compare it against all the urgent emails

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:56.560
that you found. Organize my day by

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.160
telling me what creates conflict in my

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.400
schedule, what needs an action or prep

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:04.200
before a meeting, and what can wait.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.560
This is the moment the email and

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.880
calendar start collaborating with each

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.400
other. Now, the agent starts helping me

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:15.713
shape my day. Once I run both these

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.720
email and calendar versions a few times,

00:05:15.713 --> 00:05:18.800
>> [music]

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:21.120
>> and I trust both outputs, I can schedule

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.360
the full coordination run every morning.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.800
And from that point onward, every

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.480
morning, you'll know exactly what your

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.200
day is going to look like. In Coda, you

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.280
do this by going to the schedule tab in

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.363
the sidebar or using the schedule

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.400
command inside a task. Of course,

00:05:35.363 --> 00:05:40.160
[music] be careful about scheduling

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.040
anything that touches sensitive data or

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.600
acts on your behalf without your

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:47.280
approval. And then, as you get more

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:49.680
comfortable, you can promote this agent

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:53.040
as your executive assistant. You can

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.880
prompt, "Can you block two slots for 30

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.600
minutes each so I can go running with my

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.800
buddy?" The agent will find time for you

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.920
so you can do things you want to do on

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:05.200
your busy day. So that's your

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:08.360
coordination agent. The first lesson,

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:11.280
don't delegate the decision immediately.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:14.120
First, make the work visible, then make

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.640
it efficient, then make it automatic,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:20.440
and then and only then use your judgment

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:23.760
to delegate and hand off slowly. [music]

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:25.640
And the second lesson is to not hurry

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.640
through this process.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:30.440
Start with email,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:33.840
add calendar later after you trust the

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:36.360
output. Let your agent earn higher

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.880
responsibility one step at a time. You

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.680
know, we don't get promoted easily,

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.480
neither should your agent. And by the

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.280
way, if you like these kind of

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.400
frameworks and systems thinking, I write

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.600
a newsletter that goes deep into these

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.600
themes every Tuesday. One insight, one

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:55.200
tool, one practice.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:57.000
Link is below and it's free. All right,

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.680
now it's time to move to the second

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:03.919
pillar, C for creativity. Here, you

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:06.720
bring your rough cut, notes, documents,

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:09.680
background, messy thoughts, whatever you

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.320
already have. The agent helps assemble

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.640
it into something you can judge. You're

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.160
still the director. You own the

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.919
decision. So let's do a walk-through on

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:22.560
how to build this agent. This time,

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:24.600
you're giving the agent your rough cut

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.360
and a target. It is the same prompt

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.600
skeleton from the last agent, the job,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.000
the tool, the categories, the output,

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.760
the boundary. And for this particular

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.320
agent, the output is where you focus the

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:39.200
most because that's where you're going

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:42.200
to define who it's for, how long, what

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:44.080
format, what voice. Okay, so let's use

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.840
this creativity agent to make a

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:50.320
PowerPoint presentation. I open Claude

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:53.520
Co-work on my desktop and point it to a

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.920
folder on my computer. That folder is

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.560
where I drop my rough notes and it's

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.000
where the finished deck will be created

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.320
as well. I give it the notes and tell it

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:07.200
what I want. Here are my notes for an

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:09.600
idea. I want to pitch this to the CFO.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.680
Build me a short pitch deck. Ask me

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:15.919
questions if you have any gaps in your

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:18.600
understanding. I want 8 to 10 slides and

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:20.760
I want that pitch to last for maybe 15

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:23.200
minutes. It will read my notes. It will

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.280
ask me questions if it has any. It will

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.200
even fill the gaps with a little

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:32.400
research and build the actual deck.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:35.120
What comes out is a real PowerPoint file

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:38.240
that I can open and edit in Microsoft

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.599
PowerPoint. Now, I can direct. I see

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.880
what's working, what's weak. I can tell

00:08:40.599 --> 00:08:45.400
the agent what to change.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.760
Make this longer, make this shorter,

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:50.400
make this a three bullet slide. Can we

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.839
make the opening slide cleaner? Delete

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.040
this slide. Whatever comes to mind. The

00:08:52.839 --> 00:08:57.520
agent will help you rebuild. Now, this

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:00.320
deck came out as a real file because

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:02.160
Claude uses something called a skill.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.960
That's just a set of instructions for

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.880
doing a specific job. It ships with

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:11.680
skills for PowerPoint, Word, Excel,

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:13.080
PDFs, which is why you get real files.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.920
Now, what if you want to change the look

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:17.080
and feel? Well, you can drop one of the

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:19.160
decks that you like and ask the agent to

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:21.400
match that style. Another cool way of

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:23.960
doing it is that you can even ask the

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:26.000
agent to turn your brand template into a

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:28.760
skill. From that point, the agent will

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:31.480
then always apply the same colors, the

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:33.760
same fonts, the same layout to every

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:36.200
deck and document you'll ever make. Now,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.200
this is just one example of using

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.000
skills. You can come up with 100 others.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.360
We're not just learning tools here.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.560
We're learning a new way of thinking.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.240
Now, make sure that the ultimate

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.600
direction comes from you. These agents

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.480
are your multipliers. If you come in

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:56.280
clear, AI will multiply your clarity. If

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:58.040
you come in confused, well, the AI

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:00.520
multiplies the confusion. And the best

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.520
way to create that clarity is to do a

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:04.840
lot of experiments

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:07.600
and figure out what's the best way to

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:10.720
tweak your output and even your agents.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.431
And over time, you'll build a very

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.840
strong intuition

00:10:12.431 --> 00:10:14.760
>> [music]

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:17.120
>> to get what you want. But what if you're

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:20.200
spending hours trying to understand a

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:22.440
confusing and complex document? What if

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:24.720
you need clarity to even understand

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.280
what's going on in that document? That's

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:31.040
our third time-saving pillar. C for

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:33.680
clarity. We've all signed things we

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:36.280
didn't understand. A loan contract, a

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.280
car lease, a medical report, an

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:42.680
insurance policy. It's all right there.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.760
It's correct, but it's so confusing

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:47.480
because many of these documents are

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.600
written in a language built to protect

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.800
those who wrote them. That's where the

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.440
clarity agent will save you a ton of

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:56.000
time and hassle. It's your translator,

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:57.920
and that's what we're going to build.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.440
The best way to understand what we're

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:05.040
building is to think about two scopes, a

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.280
telescope and a microscope. Sometimes

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:10.120
what you're looking for is scattered

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:12.720
across so many places that you need the

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:14.760
agent to pull it all together. And

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.200
sometimes it's the exact opposite.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:20.120
Sometimes what you're looking for is

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:23.400
buried so deep in a single dense

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:25.880
document that the agent has to dig in,

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:28.760
extract the key ideas, explain the

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:31.320
jargon, flag the risks. That is your

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:34.400
microscope. So, let's go through both of

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:37.240
them and build both ideas. Say I'm about

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:39.640
to sign a deal with a company I don't

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:41.760
know well. So, I can tell the agent,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:43.800
find out who they are. Find out

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:46.240
everything you can from the internet.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.680
Analyze our past emails with them

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.080
because, hey, we've already built our

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:53.880
coordination agent, remember? Find out

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:56.440
anything recent in the news.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:58.520
And tell me more about this deal that

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:01.480
I'm trying to strike with them. And if I

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:04.240
have uploaded documents, the agent will

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:07.560
search all my documents, all my

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:10.880
connected tools, my Google Drive, and

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:13.120
the web and come back with one picture.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:16.200
And what if I want to know more about

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:18.560
their product or competitive strategy?

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:21.280
We let the agent do the work for us. And

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:23.720
the prompt could be, now go and do deep

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.360
research on their core product from

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:30.040
reliable sources, create a document in

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:32.960
my folder, and cite the sources. Cloud

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:35.920
agents already have web search and deep

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:38.520
research built in. Same for Gemini and

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:40.880
ChatGPT. The interface may look

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:43.880
different, but it's the same idea. Two

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:47.240
phrases I use the most when I work in

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:49.920
telescope mode. Please verify,

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:52.720
and be concise. They can be super

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:56.040
helpful. Sometimes I take the research

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:58.960
output from one AI engine to another one

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:01.320
and make sure I can cross-verify. That

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:04.839
is my advisory board. And there are

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:07.200
three members in it, Claude, Gemini, and

00:13:04.839 --> 00:13:09.720
ChatGPT. And sometimes they fight among

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.640
themselves, which is great for me. Now

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:14.320
it's time to change our scope. We're

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:16.040
going to use the microscope. Let's say

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:19.360
we have a contract from the same

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:22.440
company. I uploaded the first mistake

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:24.720
most people make is asking, summarize

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:28.040
this contract. A summary makes a

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:31.160
confusing document shorter, sure. But in

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:34.320
legal contracts or long policies, the

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:38.080
devil is always in the details. So

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:40.000
instead, my prompt would be, "Read this

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:42.960
document carefully.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.760
Find the key terms like fee structure

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:48.160
and obligations and deadlines and

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:51.640
exclusions and risks.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:54.720
Pay attention to anything that's unclear

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:57.520
or anything that makes me liable."

00:13:54.720 --> 00:14:00.080
Create a table with five columns. What

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:03.800
the contract says, what it means in

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:06.760
plain English, why it matters, the risk

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:09.240
level, and the questions I should be

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:11.200
asking. Again, that's just one of many

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:13.600
prompts you can give it, but you get the

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.600
point. You want to break that document

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.480
open, not get a bird's-eye view on it.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:22.000
That's what agents can do for you. And

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:25.760
now, one more important thing, your

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:28.400
mileage may vary, but I personally don't

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:32.040
like uploading sensitive information

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:35.000
like personal financial data or medical

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:37.320
reports. So, be mindful about all of

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:40.160
that. All right, we're on to the fourth

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:42.520
pillar. This last agent helps you handle

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:45.120
the moment once you're in the room, the

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:47.280
interview, the raise, the pitch. With

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:49.680
some conversations, you only get one

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:52.480
shot. That's why you have to rehearse. I

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:55.400
remember my first job interview. It was

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:58.560
long time ago. And I had no idea what to

00:14:55.400 --> 00:15:00.600
do, what to wear. I didn't even know how

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:03.800
to tie a tie.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:06.600
I don't think I even owned a tie. So, I

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:09.800
asked my roommate if I could borrow his

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:11.320
tie. His name was Mike Green. Awesome

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:14.680
awesome roommate.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:16.000
He said, "Sure, but do you know how to

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:18.400
wear it?"

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:21.440
And I shook my head and we both cracked

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:24.120
up. So, he got a tie, tied it around his

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:26.960
neck first, loosened it, and handed it

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:28.800
to me and said, "Good luck."

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:30.920
Of course, I walked in wearing a

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:33.160
borrowed tie, but still had the same

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:34.680
anxiety about my first interview. Turns

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:37.680
out that when it comes to interview

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:40.800
anxiety, I'm not alone. In a survey done

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:42.720
by JDP, 93%

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:45.280
of people said they get anxious about

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:47.280
job interviews. That's almost all of us.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:50.000
But the survey also said something very

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:52.400
useful. One of the best ways to reduce

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.760
that anxiety is to practice that

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:57.360
interview out loud. That's the whole

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:59.680
point of the coaching agent. The first

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:01.560
time you say the most important thing,

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:03.880
you should not be in the most important

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:07.040
room. We are going to build an agent

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:10.000
that helps you rehearse. I know CEOs who

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:13.080
rehearse their entire board meeting with

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.640
six or seven agents, where each agent is

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:17.800
designed to mimic the personality of a

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:20.560
specific board member.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:22.840
Yeah. It's that important. We will use

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:25.080
job interview as an example as we build

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:27.560
it, but you can use it for any

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:30.960
professional or personal conversation or

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:33.680
negotiations. Okay, first, I add all the

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:37.560
context, the company background, job

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:39.720
description, my cover letter, my resume,

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:42.600
my position statement, [music] and so

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:45.920
on. Second, we want to tell the agent

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:48.360
who to be. You are the hiring manager

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:51.600
for a senior product role. You are

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:54.160
sharp. You're a little skeptical.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:56.600
And you've read my resume, so go ahead

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:59.680
and interview me. Ask one question at a

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:02.640
time. And push back on weak answers like

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:05.120
a real interviewer would. And keep track

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:08.240
of our entire interview so we can

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:11.240
analyze and improve my performance. And

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:14.280
here's the move that makes it all real.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:17.600
Don't type it. Talk to it. Turn on the

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:20.120
voice mode. On your phone. So ChatGPT,

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:22.439
Gemini, Claude, they all let you speak

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:24.600
out loud, and they all can have natural

00:17:22.439 --> 00:17:26.600
conversation with you. You can interrupt

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:29.480
it, you can ask clarifying questions.

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:32.080
It's a real conversation. I do this all

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:34.840
the time on my walks. And when I'm done,

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:37.760
I want both the agent and me to break

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:40.200
character. I would say, "Okay, we're

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:42.640
done with the interview. Now, you're my

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:43.720
interview coach. Tell me where I was

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:45.800
weak,

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:47.920
where I fumbled,

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:50.480
what I should have said.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:52.520
Tell me where I rambled on. Give me

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:54.520
three ways I could have answered that

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:57.640
question better." And once the coaching

00:17:54.520 --> 00:18:01.240
session is over, now you can turn up the

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:04.280
heat. Give that agent a persona of a

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:06.840
tougher hiring manager. Maybe you're

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:09.000
applying to a startup, and the CEO will

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:11.880
be the one interviewing you at the end.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:14.080
Give that persona to the agent. Let the

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:17.040
agent know that it should conduct the

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:19.080
interview in that persona for 45

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:22.320
minutes, and give you the last 15

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:25.080
minutes to ask questions to the CEO.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:27.440
Because that will happen in real life.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:28.760
And finally, once you're done with all

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:31.520
your reps,

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:34.720
don't forget to ask. Turn what you've

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:37.080
learned into a one-page prep card, so I

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:38.560
can review it before the interview. Now,

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:42.120
you have something you can use. [music]

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:44.600
Your goal here is not to create a set of

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:47.600
scripted answers, but to build the

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:50.200
capacity to deliver good answers, no

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:53.280
matter what the questions are. Like all

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:56.360
great musicians, rehearsals teach you to

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:59.200
build judgment and taste, so you can

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:02.000
become comfortable improvising on stage.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:06.640
So, those are the four pillars. You

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:08.880
know, as AI becomes more entangled in

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:11.240
our lives, there's definitely a sense of

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:14.440
fear running under all of this.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:18.160
According to a recent survey, 70%

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:21.480
of Americans believe AI will shrink job

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:26.480
opportunities. And among Gen Z, it is

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:28.760
81%. Sure, some jobs will be displaced.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:30.040
There will definitely be some pain

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:33.560
around that.

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:37.240
But, many, many more new jobs will be

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:40.560
created over time because of AI.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:44.120
We have been here before.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:46.440
Every age has believed that the machine

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:48.680
would swallow the person.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:51.840
Charlie Chaplin showed it in Modern

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:54.360
Times. A man literally pulled into the

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.960
gears of a factory. And that was made in

00:19:54.360 --> 00:20:00.360
1936.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:20:04.040
If the contest is speed, memory, or

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:05.400
faster output, machines will win every

00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:07.840
single time.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:11.560
But, I think the real contest will be

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:13.200
about the things machines cannot own.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:15.000
Your attention,

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:17.080
your creativity,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:19.440
your clarity,

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:21.440
and that one thing that matters most

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.920
when you build your career, your life,

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:25.120
your company,

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:27.080
your judgment.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:29.560
Those are the four pillars we talked

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:29.560
about.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:34.920
Sure, the machines will keep getting

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:36.600
smarter and faster.

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:38.680
Let them.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:42.080
Use the machines.

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:42.080
You don't have to become one.

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:47.920
See you next week. Thank you.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:47.920
And I love you.

